{{also|Prudent}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|prudent}}, from {{der|en|fro|prudent}}, from {{der|en|la|prūdēns}}, contracted from {{m|la|prōvidēns||foresight}} ({{cog|en|providence}}), the past participle of {{m|la|prōvideō|t=I forsee}}. Unrelated to {{m|en|prude}}. {{doublet|en|provident}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɹuːdənt/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-prudent.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːdənt|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[sagacious|Sagacious]] in adapting means to ends; [[circumspect]] in action, or in determining any line of conduct.
#: {{syn|en|careful|considerate|discreet|Thesaurus:cautious}}
#* {{quote-book
|1=en
|year=1864
|author=Jules Verne
|title=Around the World in 80 Days
|chapter=30
|url=http://ebooks.adelaide.edu.au/v/verne/jules/v52aw/chapter30.html
|passage=He did not hesitate what to do. It would be '''prudent''' to continue on to Omaha, for it would be dangerous to return to the train, which the Indians might still be engaged in pillaging.
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120412180700/http://ebooks.adelaide.edu.au/v/verne/jules/v52aw/chapter30.html
|archivedate=12 April 2012
}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1643|author=w:John Milton|title=Doctrine and Discipline of Divorce
|passage=Moses established a grave and '''prudent''' law.}}
# Practically [[wise]], [[judicious]], [[shrewd]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:wise}}
#: {{ux|en|His '''prudent''' career moves reliably brought him to the top.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Proverbs|22|3|passage=A '''prudent''' man foreseeth the euill, and hideth himselfe: but the simple passe on, and are punished.}}
# [[frugal|Frugal]], [[economical]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:frugal}}
#: {{ant|en|extravagant}}
#: {{ux|en|Only '''prudent''' expenditure may provide quality within a restrictive budget.}}

